                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %p
            That is,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \vee q
            is true if and only if at least one of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            is true. Disjunction also exists in nearly every non-classical logic.
          %p
            More generally, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            are any two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relations"} relations
            on the same domain, then we define their disjunction pointwise, thinking of a relation as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            to truth values. If instead we think of a relation as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            of its domain, then disjunction becomes
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/union"} union
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
            for disjunction are given as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi P
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} prop
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi Q
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} prop
