                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash P \; \mathrm{prop} \quad \Gamma \vdash Q \; \mathrm{prop} \quad \Gamma, P \vee Q \; \mathrm{true} \vdash R \; \mathrm{prop} \quad \Gamma, P \; \mathrm{true} \vdash R \; \mathrm{true} \quad \Gamma, Q \; \mathrm{true} \vdash R \; \mathrm{true}}{\Gamma, P \vee Q \; \mathrm{true} \vdash R \; \mathrm{true}}
          %h2#remarks Remarks
          %p
            Disjunction as defined above is sometimes called
            %strong inclusive disjunction
            to distinguish it from
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exclusive+disjunction"} exclusive disjunction
            %em exactly
            one of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            must be true.
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substructural+logics"} substructural logics
            such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+disjunction"} additive disjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊕
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus
            and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+disjunction"} multiplicative disjunction
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⅋
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \parr
            %a{:href => "#RoI"} Rules of Inference
            below for the distinction. In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join"} join
            under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entailment"} entailment
            relation, just like disjunction in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            (and
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
          %p
            Disjunction is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Morgan+dual"} de Morgan dual
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+conjunction"} conjunction
          %p
            Like any join, disjunction is an associative operation, so we can take the disjunction of any finite positive whole number of truth values; the disjunction is true if and only if at least one of the various truth values is true. Disjunction also has an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+element"} identity element
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/falsity"} false
            truth value. Some logics allow a notion of infinitary disjunction. Indexed disjunction is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantification"} existential quantification
          %h2#in_dependent_type_theory In dependent type theory
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mere+propositions"} mere propositions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bracket+type"} bracket type
            of their
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum+type"} sum type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi P
                    %mo +
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \| P + Q \|
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+type"} higher inductive type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coq"} Coq
            syntax:
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                Inductive disjunction (P Q:Type) : Type :=
                | inl : P -> disjunction P Q
                | inr : Q -> disjunction P Q
                | contr0 : forall (p q : disjunction P Q) p == q          %p
            If the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+propositions"} type of propositions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Prop}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            \-
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo :
                        %mi U
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isProp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{A:U} \mathrm{isProp}(A)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is defined as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+function+type"} dependent function type
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi B
                  %mo ≡
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo :
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
